      "date": "0037-03-16",
      "headline": "Caligula Becomes Emperor",
      "text": "Following the death of Tiberius, Caligula was declared Emperor of Rome on March 16, 37 AD. His ascension was initially met with widespread approval."
    },
    {
      "date": "0037-10",
      "headline": "Illness and Change in Behavior",
      "text": "In October 37 AD, Caligula fell seriously ill. After recovering, his behavior reportedly changed, becoming more erratic and autocratic."
    },
    {
      "date": "0038",
      "headline": "Execution of Political Rivals",
      "text": "Caligula began executing political rivals and perceived threats, including his cousin and adopted son, Gemellus, and his brother-in-law, Marcus Lepidus."
    },
    {
      "date": "0039",
      "headline": "Military Campaigns and Financial Strain",
      "text": "Caligula launched several military campaigns, including a planned invasion of Britain, which strained the empire's finances."
    },
    {
      "date": "0040",
      "headline": "Declared Himself a Living God",
      "text": "Caligula declared himself a living god and began appearing in public dressed as various deities, demanding to be worshipped."
    },
