      "headline": "Birth of Oskar Schindler",
      "text": "Oskar Schindler was born on April 28, 1908, in Zwittau, Moravia, which was then part of the Austro-Hungarian Empire (now Svitavy in the Czech Republic)."
    },
    {
      "date": "1936",
      "headline": "Joined the Nazi Party",
      "text": "Oskar Schindler joined the Nazi Party in 1936, motivated by business opportunities and the political climate of the time."
    },
    {
      "date": "1939",
      "headline": "Acquisition of Emalia Factory",
      "text": "In 1939, Schindler acquired an enamelware factory in Kraków, Poland, which he named Deutsche Emailwarenfabrik (DEF). This factory would later become a haven for Jewish workers during the Holocaust."
    },
    {
      "date": "1942",
      "headline": "Start of Employing Jewish Workers",
      "text": "By 1942, Schindler began employing Jewish workers from the Kraków Ghetto, initially to capitalize on cheap labor, but later to protect them from deportation and death."
    },
    {
      "date": "1944",
      "headline": "Creation of Schindler's List",
      "text": "In 1944, Schindler compiled a list of over 1,200 Jewish workers to be transferred to his new factory in Brünnlitz, Czechoslovakia, effectively saving them from the Holocaust."
    },
    {
      "date": "1945",
      "headline": "End of World War II",
      "text": "With the end of World War II in 1945, Schindler's efforts to save his Jewish workers were recognized, and he fled to avoid capture by the advancing Soviet forces."
    },
    {
      "date": "1962",
      "headline": "Righteous Among the Nations",
      "text": "In 1962, Oskar Schindler was recognized by Yad Vashem as Righteous Among the Nations for his efforts to save Jews during the Holocaust."
    },
    {
      "date": "1974-10-09",
      "headline": "Death of Oskar Schindler",
      "text": "Oskar Schindler died on October 9, 1974, in Hildesheim, West Germany. He was buried in Jerusalem on Mount Zion, the only member of the Nazi Party to be honored in this way."
    }
  ]
}

