      "text": "The Bhagavad Gita is believed to have been composed around the second century BCE. It is a 700-verse Hindu scripture that is part of the Indian epic Mahabharata, specifically the Bhishma Parva (Book of Bhishma)."
    },
    {
      "date": "-0200",
      "headline": "Integration into the Mahabharata",
      "text": "The Bhagavad Gita was integrated into the Mahabharata, an ancient Indian epic that narrates the Kurukshetra War and the fates of the Kaurava and the Pandava princes."
    },
    {
      "date": "-0200",
      "headline": "Philosophical Synthesis",
      "text": "The Bhagavad Gita synthesizes various strands of Indian religious thought, including the Vedic concept of dharma, samkhya-based yoga and jnana (knowledge), and bhakti (devotion)."
    },
    {
      "date": "-0200",
      "headline": "Influence on Hindu Philosophy",
      "text": "The Bhagavad Gita holds a unique pan-Hindu influence as the most prominent sacred text and is a central text in Vedanta and the Vaishnava Hindu tradition."
    },
    {
      "date": "1785",
      "headline": "First English Translation",
      "text": "The Bhagavad Gita was first translated into English by Charles Wilkins, marking the beginning of its introduction to the Western world."
    },
    {
      "date": "1885",
      "headline": "Swami Vivekananda's Interpretation",
      "text": "Swami Vivekananda, a key figure in introducing Indian philosophies to the Western world, provided interpretations of the Bhagavad Gita, emphasizing its teachings on selfless action and duty."
    },
    {
      "date": "1944",
      "headline": "Mahatma Gandhi's Commentary",
      "text": "Mahatma Gandhi published his commentary on the Bhagavad Gita, titled 'Anasakti Yoga,' which highlighted the text's teachings on non-attachment and selfless action."
    },
    {
      "date": "1968",
      "headline": "Publication of 'Bhagavad-gītā As It Is'",
      "text": "A. C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada published 'Bhagavad-gītā As It Is,' a widely read translation and commentary that became central to the International Society for Krishna Consciousness (ISKCON)."
    }
  ]
}

