      "text": "Temüjin is proclaimed Genghis Khan, the universal ruler of the Mongols, at a kurultai (assembly) of Mongol chiefs. This marks the official founding of the Mongol Empire."
    },
    {
      "date": "1211",
      "headline": "Invasion of the Jin Dynasty",
      "text": "The Mongol Empire begins its invasion of the Jin Dynasty in northern China, marking the start of a series of campaigns that would eventually lead to the conquest of northern China."
    },
    {
      "date": "1219",
      "headline": "Invasion of the Khwarazmian Empire",
      "text": "Genghis Khan launches a campaign against the Khwarazmian Empire in Central Asia after a diplomatic incident, leading to the empire's eventual destruction."
    },
    {
      "date": "1227",
      "headline": "Death of Genghis Khan",
      "text": "Genghis Khan dies during a campaign against the Western Xia. His death leads to the division of the empire among his sons and grandsons."
    },
    {
      "date": "1237",
      "headline": "Invasion of Europe",
      "text": "The Mongols begin their invasion of Europe, starting with the conquest of the Rus' principalities, and later moving into Hungary and Poland."
    },
    {
      "date": "1258",
      "headline": "Sack of Baghdad",
      "text": "The Mongols capture and sack Baghdad, leading to the end of the Islamic Golden Age and the destruction of the Abbasid Caliphate."
    },
    {
      "date": "1260",
      "headline": "Battle of Ain Jalut",
      "text": "The Mongols are defeated by the Mamluks in the Battle of Ain Jalut in present-day Israel, marking the first major defeat of the Mongol Empire and halting their advance into Africa."
    },
    {
      "date": "1271",
      "headline": "Establishment of the Yuan Dynasty",
      "text": "Kublai Khan, a grandson of Genghis Khan, establishes the Yuan Dynasty in China, marking the first time the entire country is ruled by a non-Han dynasty."
    },
    {
      "date": "1279",
      "headline": "Conquest of the Song Dynasty",
      "text": "The Mongols complete their conquest of China with the defeat of the Southern Song Dynasty, unifying China under the Yuan Dynasty."
    },
    {
      "date": "1294",
      "headline": "Death of Kublai Khan",
      "text": "Kublai Khan dies, leading to a period of instability and decline in the Mongol Empire as his successors struggle to maintain control."
    },
    {
      "date": "1368",
      "headline": "End of the Yuan Dynasty",
      "text": "The Yuan Dynasty is overthrown by the Ming Dynasty, marking the end of Mongol rule in China and the fragmentation of the Mongol Empire."
    }
  ]
}

